window.__posterData({"id": "ege-erdal", "name": "Ege Erdal", "folder": "Week44_AI_Powered_Poster_EgeErdal_2026_10_30_13_08_43", "v": "2", "scripts": ["https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/p5@2.3.4/lib/p5.js", "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/p5.sound@0.4.1/dist/p5.sound.min.js"], "links": [], "css": "html, body {\n  margin: 0;\n  padding: 0;\n}\ncanvas {\n  display: block;\n}\n", "code": "\n/*\nWeek 44 process notes:\n\nFrom week 42 I kept the 600x800 canvas, photograph, Slowji font, RAVE title, fade, and RGB filter. The moving blocks I requested and the AI's scan line build on the original glitch idea by making the filter's edge distortion more visible. These are all core principles of the design and this week's work is simply aiming to enhance what is already there.\n\nStep 1:\nI asked the AI to refactor the original sketch without changing its\nbehavior. It polished the formatting and moved the existing rectangle loop into its own drawGlitchBlocks() function. It also changed \"==\" to \"===\" since phase is always a number here. That change does not alter this sketch's behavior. This triple equals was however not adressed during the course and therefore I will quickly go over it; it is a strict equality that checks for if values are equal AND if they are the same type. This essentially makes the code more consistent. The lines got longer after the refactoring; from 122 to 137 lines.\n\nA reasoning for the code getting longer might aswell just be that we didn't tell it to shorten the code. Refactoring in itself means efficiently reorganizing and optimizing code for readability and making it \"cleaner\" in a sense. Making the code longer was simply needed for that little extra in my code as it lacked proper structuring when it comes to identation and closing bracket placements. Sure the code works, but was it optimal? If we were to put me in a scenario with code developers - what would they prefer?\n\nI believe the answer truly lies in that those changes create a quality of life that AI envisioned and optimized, helping me therefore realize the value by actually having an LLM show me how the code could be shaped differently. Personally this has been quite giving since learning and applying it myself is different to getting a demonstration applied to my own personal work. \n\nStep 2:\nI went with what I got on the first try since I was contempt with the results. I had asked the AI to make it so for each click during the rave to create a glitch block that moves horizontally and vertically. The block now stores its own speed, and the loop updates each block's position and reverses it at the canvas edges. This really is nice since I planned to utilize the edges.\n\nStep 3:\nThe AI chose to add a cyan and pink laser scan that moves behind the rave title. The design reason was to reuse the poster's existing colors and RGB split rather than to introduce a new and unfamiliar visual style.\n\nMy own design critique for this is that I find the scan line really \"in line\" with my original rave vision.It feels like a natural part of the design. At first, I considered removing the filter during my initial work during week 42 because its effect seemed subtle during the rave. The filter's distortion is easiest to notice near the edges and corners. When moving blocks reach the corners and the scan line reaches\nthe top or bottom, those elements appear elongated and enlarged. This makes the original filter valuable to m since it connects the new motion to the glitch theme and makes the poster feel closer to my what i envisioned with the glitches.\n\nI believe the balance is still held even with the rectangles moving in xy and the line that moves in y. To my design-brain both visually and in terms of interactivity it appears intentional even though the blocks are drawn over the title. There is a clear line of thought present going from inanimate blocks in the original version to moving pixels and lines which embody the overall aesthetic well. In this project it enriches the vision of rave. \n\n\nIn all honesty - I believe the moving rectangles and possibly the line would be within my own reach to achieve during the initial poster-assignment with an extended due-date. \n*/\n\n\nlet img;\nlet displaceColors;\nlet font;\nlet phase = 0; // 0 = poster, 1 = fade out, 2 = rave\nlet posterAlpha = 255;\nlet glitchBlocks = []; // Stores rectangles added during the rave\n\nasync function setup() {\n  createCanvas(600, 800, WEBGL);\n\n  img = await loadImage('david-schultz-vIDPiYxBuxk-unsplash.jpg');\n  font = await loadFont('SlowjiRegular-vne9Z.otf');\n\n  displaceColors = buildFilterShader(displaceColorsCallback);\n}\n\nfunction displaceColorsCallback() {\n  function zoom(coord, amount) {\n    return (coord - 0.5) / amount + 0.5;\n  } \n  \n  filterColor.begin();\n  const uv = filterColor.texCoord;\n\n  // Sample the color channels at slightly different positions for the RGB split.\n  const r = getTexture(filterColor.canvasContent, zoom(uv, 0.6)).r;\n  const g = getTexture(filterColor.canvasContent, zoom(uv, 0.7)).g;\n  const b = getTexture(filterColor.canvasContent, zoom(uv, 0.6)).b;\n  const a = getTexture(filterColor.canvasContent, uv).a;\n\n  filterColor.set([r, g, b, a]);\n  filterColor.end();\n}\n\nfunction mousePressed() {\n  if (phase === 0) {\n    phase = 1; // The first click starts the poster fade.\n  } else if (phase === 2) {\n    // Save one new rectangle so it can be drawn in every frame.\n    glitchBlocks.push({\n      x: mouseX - width / 2,\n      y: mouseY - height / 2,\n      w: random(30, 100),\n      h: random(8, 30),\n      // From step 2 request: each new block moves horizontally and vertically. AI made this one at my request to move the blocks once they have been deployed. \n      speedX: random(-2, 2), // Horizontal speed in pixels per frame\n      speedY: random(-2, 2), // Vertical speed in pixels per frame\n      col: color(random(255), random(255), random(255), 180)\n    });\n  }\n}\n/* These brackets and the indentation now are a clear example of some of the formatting changes the AI made in the refractured code step 1. I did not bother with spacing and making it look better in my original work. This makes me think I should have shown more care. In my original work it's just like this:\n\nglitchBlocks.push({ //push saves stuff to the array, this becomes an \"object\". p5js:s data structure garden gives a good tutorial kinda\nx: mouseX - width / 2,\ny: mouseY - height / 2,\nw: random(30, 100),\nh: random(8, 30),\ncol: color(random(255), random(255), random(255), 180) // creates  rectangles during phase 2\n});\n}\n}  \n\nIn the original work these brackets and the properties were like flies stuck on a wall against the left margin. Now you can see isn't flushed against the left marginal. The refactor actually made them indented under the object assigned which is massive improvement in terms of organising. I believe this not only makes it easier for me as a coder ut also easier for an outsider reviewing the code since the nesting is easier to see.\n*/\nfunction draw() {\n  background(0);\n\n  if (phase === 0) {\n    drawPoster(255);\n  } else if (phase === 1) {\n    posterAlpha = max(0, posterAlpha - 3);\n    drawPoster(posterAlpha);\n\n    if (posterAlpha === 0) {\n      phase = 2;\n    }\n  }\n\n  if (phase === 2) {\n    drawLaserSweep(); // From step 3; the AI suggestion: a moving neon scan behind the rave.\n    drawRave();\n  }\n\n  filter(displaceColors);\n  // so this filter actually runs every frame, thing is that it is significantly easier to see the distortion near the edges where the shader samples RGB values from different positions. It's not switched on by clicking, it's there through all the phases.\n}\n\nfunction drawLaserSweep() {\n  // From step 3. The sin() moves the beam smoothly between the top and bottom of the canvas. Essentially it used the increasing frame count to produce a smooth repeating value between -1 and 1. The map converts that range into the canvas' vertical coordinates (-400 to 400). The link from p5.js does a pretty well job of explaing it. Every link can be found down below.\n  let laserY = map(sin(frameCount * 0.025), -1, 1, -height / 2, height / 2);\n\n  push();\n  stroke(0, 255, 250, 70);\n  strokeWeight(18);\n  line(-width / 2, laserY, width / 2, laserY);\n\n  stroke(255, 0, 80, 180);\n  strokeWeight(2);\n  line(-width / 2, laserY - 2, width / 2, laserY - 2);\n  pop();\n}\n\nfunction drawPoster(opacity) {\n  push();\n  imageMode(CENTER);\n  tint(255, opacity);\n  image(img, 0, 0, width, height, 0, 0, img.width, img.height, COVER);\n  noTint();\n  pop();\n}\n\nfunction drawRave() {\n  // Draw the artist line and the three offset title layers.\n  push();\n  textFont(font);\n  textSize(40);\n  textAlign(LEFT, BASELINE);\n  noStroke();\n\n  fill(255, 0, 80, 127);\n  text(\n    'Ege Erdal | Rave To The Grave',\n    -290 + random(-15, -12),\n    300 + random(-5, -2)\n  );\n\n  textSize(200);\n\n  fill(255, 0, 80, 127);\n  text('RAVE', -260 + random(-15, 15), -250 + random(-5, 5));\n\n  fill(0, 255, 250, 127);\n  text('RAVE', -260 + random(-15, 15), -250 + random(-5, 5));\n\n  fill(250, 209, 61);\n  text('RAVE', -260, -250);\n  pop();\n\n  drawGlitchBlocks();\n}\n\nfunction drawGlitchBlocks() { // From step 1, as you can see here it got its own function thanks to AI, moving it from drawRave() to here seems purely for organisation.\n  push();\n  rectMode(CENTER);\n  noStroke();\n\n  // From the first step refactor: the original rectangle loop is in its own function.\n  // Visits each saved rectangle by its index and draws it.\n  for (let i = 0; i < glitchBlocks.length; i++) {\n    let block = glitchBlocks[i];\n\n    // From step 2 - this moves the block a little in both directions each frame. \"+=\" essentially means \"add this amount to the current value\"\n    block.x += block.speedX;\n    block.y += block.speedY;\n\n    // From step number 2, this reverses direction when its center reaches a poster edge.\n    if (block.x < -width / 2 || block.x > width / 2) {\n      block.speedX *= -1;\n    }\n    if (block.y < -height / 2 || block.y > height / 2) {\n      block.speedY *= -1;\n    }\n\n    fill(block.col);\n    rect(block.x, block.y, block.w, block.h);\n  }\n\n  pop();\n}\n\n// Filter adapted from the p5.js filter shader example:\n// https://p5js.org/examples/3D-Filter-Shader-p5strands/\n\n// p5.js references used in the sketch:\n// https://p5js.org/reference/p5/rectMode/\n// https://v1.p5js.org/tutorials/repeating-with-loops/\n// https://p5js.org/reference/p5/max/\n// https://p5js.org/reference/p5/push/\n// https://p5js.org/es/tutorials/data-structure-garden/\n// p5.js references for the moving laser:\n// https://p5js.org/reference/p5/sin/\n// https://p5js.org/reference/p5/map/\n// https://p5js.org/reference/p5/frameCount/\n", "assets": {"SlowjiRegular-vne9Z.otf": {"mime": "font/otf", "b64": ""}, "david-schultz-vIDPiYxBuxk-unsplash.jpg": {"mime": "image/webp", "b64": ""}}});
